count': '135', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="http://twitter.com/download/iphone" rel="nofollow">Twitter for iPhone</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4NzczOTEzNjM4Njk5MTMwODk=', 'text': 'I might have believed that car emissions were about protecting the air until I found out that California will not let you replace an old engine with a brand new engine in an old car that can easily pass an emissions test. \n\nInstead, California state government insists that you purchase an entirely new car which is not compliant with reduce, reuse, recycle. \n\nThis leads me to believe that these policies are better explained by unions who may manufacture cars, or some other explanation. \n\nIf these policies were actually about clean air, you could replace the old engine with a brand new engine in an old car to meet emissions requirements, and this would result in cleaner air while also adhering to the principles of reduce, reuse, recycle.\n\nSomeone else may make an argument that it’s about driver safety, and making sure that drivers have newer cars with airbags. \n\nMy response to that would be that 1) I’m not asking other people to drive my car. 2) I’m not forcing anybody to ride in my car. 3) I’m the one paying the repair bills on that old car. 4) My old car or truck is not a public transit vehicle. It is a private vehicle for private use funded by private funding. It’s either emissions compliant or it isn’t.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Thu Jan 09 16:25:56 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1877157209445900313', 'display_text_range': [14, 291], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}]}, 'favorite_count': 2, 'favorited': Fal